;; A fixed-length dataset of one record type whose table holds a redefine no
;; byte of the record chooses between, read noodoslide -- a constant four
;; entries, the count an ordinary item beside them.
;;
;; The two discriminations are in the two scopes and only one of them is a
;; discrimination at all. The first chooses a record and is evaluated before
;; anything has been admitted. The second settles which alternative an entry of
;; the table carries, and it tests nothing: entry one is the home address,
;; entries two and three are work addresses and entry four is the mailing
;; address, which is a fact about the file the adopter has and not one any item
;; of a record states.
;;
;; A schedule is what that shape needs. The count sits outside an occurrence, so
;; a predicate reading it would choose one arm for every entry of the record; an
;; arm carries no guard; and the strategies admit nothing that tests neither. So
;; the alternative is decided by where an entry sits, which is the position the
;; arms are written against here.
;;
;; The count in this file is not the number of entries the table holds. Under
;; this reading the table is always four entries wide and the count says how
;; many of them a program filled in, which is what makes the reading visible:
;; the second record says two and carries four, so a consumer that read the
;; table at the count would take ADR-TAIL out of the third entry's bytes.
(encoding
  (charset ascii)
  (sign-convention ascii-zone-37)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

(framing
  (recfm F)
  (lrecl 34))

(record ADDRESS
  (copybook "address.cpy" ADR-RECORD))

(copybook-reading
  (occurs-depending-on noodoslide))

(discriminate ADDRESS (equals (item ADDRESS ADR-TYPE) "A"))

(schedule-variant (item ADDRESS ADR-ENTRY ADR-HOME)
  (arm ADR-HOME 1)
  (arm ADR-WORK 2 3)
  (arm ADR-MAIL 4))

(sequence (* ADDRESS))
